<Base
  lang="en"
  page="overview"
  eyebrow="A home system, built for a hackathon"
  title="The home system"
  description="Lanternina runs parent-approved activities on paper and e-paper. A handheld camera returns completed sheets. Language, vision and image inference runs in Microsoft Foundry."
>
  <p class="lede">Lanternina invents an activity for an adolescent, has a parent approve it, and then runs it in a real room, using paper, e-paper displays and physical buttons. It reads completed sheets returned through the camera or scanner, uses the additions to continue, and stops at an hour the parent set.</p>

  <p>
    The mini-PC acts as a hub on the home network and can sit out of sight near the router. Displays are separate objects placed around the rooms: they communicate with the hub over Wi-Fi, without cables connecting them to the mini-PC. The printer and scanner are also reached over the network. The hub manages the clock and requests pages, readings and continuations from the cloud service.
  </p>

  <p>The displays run on batteries and can be placed where they are useful; more can be added. Displays and portable devices are designed for very low power consumption, aiming for weeks of availability between charges. They spend much of their time asleep and wake to communicate; e-paper retains its image while asleep. Battery life depends on update frequency and use. The hub and printer use a fixed power supply.</p>

  <figure class="band">
    <img
      src="/images/hero.jpg"
      width="1800"
      height="771"
      alt="A 7.5-inch e-paper display in a 3D printed frame, showing an engraving."
    />
