  <h2>How an activity goes</h2>

  <p>
    The hub requests activities to offer. A parent can also prepare a brief in the panel. The generated, checked activity waits for a decision before the hub starts it.
  </p>

  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <b>The parent steers the proposal.</b> They write interests, subjects to avoid, language and current circumstances. They can prepare a brief and set the available hours.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>The container generates the activity.</b> The router sends household material and the chosen form and move. The response contains moments, three durations for each, help and ways out.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>The API checks and stores the activity.</b> An unreadable answer can receive a format repair. Structural complaints can trigger a separate repair. Remaining complaints stop the request; Content Safety screens accepted text. The parent can approve or refuse the offered activity.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>The mini-PC collects the activity.</b> It polls the device API on its own schedule. The API
      returns the waiting activity in that HTTPS response, and the mini-PC stores it locally before
      preparing the first page.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>The mini-PC requests a page from the container and prints it.</b> The image model receives the page contract, not the whole script. The container returns the drawing; the hub sends it to the printer over IPP.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>The camera or scanner returns the completed page.</b> A vision model receives the photograph or scan and the original page image. It compares them and returns a reading of the marks that were added.
    </li>
    <li>
      <b>The reading selects the next moment.</b> The execution service follows the corresponding
      edge in the activity graph and prepares the next display or page. Thirty minutes before the
      hour set by the parent, it enters the planned route from the current moment to the ending.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="camera">Returning the sheet with the camera</h2>
  <p>The adolescent photographs the completed sheet to return it to the activity. The camera uses a XIAO ESP32S3 Sense in a 3D-printed enclosure, with a shutter button, a feedback light and a battery. The prototype produces JPEGs at 1600 × 1200 pixels.</p>

  <figure>
    <a href="/images/camera-prototype-2.webp"><img src="/images/camera-prototype-2.webp" width="1600" height="1200" loading="lazy" alt="The assembled white camera, with its lens on the front and a green shutter button and yellow feedback light on top." /></a>
    <figcaption>The assembled prototype, photographed with an iPhone on 9 September 2026.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>A press starts acquisition. The light stays on until the selected frame is ready; saving and delivery continue after it goes out. The firmware writes the JPEG to local flash and reads it back before starting Wi-Fi. It sends the image to the home hub over authenticated HTTPS. Two flashes confirm a matching receipt after the hub has stored it. A failed delivery keeps the photograph and its identifier for a later attempt.</p>
  <p>The reader receives the photograph and the exact original page kept for printing. It compares them despite changes in perspective and lighting, transcribes legible additions and reports uncertainty. An uncertain reading or an ambiguous association with an activity leaves the activity waiting. The scanner remains available for the same return. The family can view, download and delete the photographs in the parent's panel.</p>

  <figure class="narrow">
    <a href="/images/camera-prototype-1.webp"><img src="/images/camera-prototype-1.webp" width="1200" height="1600" loading="lazy" alt="The open camera enclosure showing the controller board, antenna, wiring, shutter and battery beside it." /></a>
    <figcaption>The enclosure during assembly on 9 September 2026. This iPhone photograph documents that assembly; it is not a wiring diagram.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>Battery-powered shutter, light feedback, sleep/wake cycles and delivery were exercised on 9 September. USB tests also verified that a photograph survives an unavailable receiver and is delivered with the same identifier when it returns. <a href="/en/status/#camera-checks">The implementation page records the measured results and remaining trials.</a></p>

  <h2>The parent's panel</h2>

  <p>The parent opens <a href="https://app.lanternina.com">the panel</a> in a browser. Waiting activities show their title, duration, planned sheets and a description of what will happen. The parent can read the script, approve, refuse or work on the idea. Approval makes the activity available to the house without starting it immediately.</p>

  <figure>
    <a href="/images/panel-activities-en.webp"><img src="/images/panel-activities-en.webp" width="1200" height="900" loading="lazy" alt="The activity panel showing a proposed activity, its expandable script and the Approve, Refuse and Work on it controls." /></a>
    <figcaption>Activities awaiting approval, with invented data. The sample activity is in Italian.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>Settings cover days and hours, how many activities may begin, interests, subjects to avoid and language. The parent can add a temporary note and set bounds on how a continuation may change. During an activity, they can bring its end time forward or ask it to start closing. The activity log collects what the system generated and showed.</p>

  <h3>Devices in the house</h3>
  <p>The panel lists registered displays and discovered printers and scanners. The parent names them, assigns jobs and sees which need attention. Several devices can hold the same job, and one device can hold several jobs.</p>

  <figure>
    <a href="/images/panel-devices-en.webp"><img src="/images/panel-devices-en.webp" width="1200" height="900" loading="lazy" alt="The panel's Devices page with editable names, display status and checkboxes for assigning jobs." /></a>
    <figcaption>Device names and jobs, with invented data.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Reconstructing a railway</h2>

  <p>
    An old railway connected a hut to a cutting, passing between rows of trees and over a bridge. The map preserves these observations but leaves the route beside the pond uncertain. The activity asks for a possible route in the notebook, its direction and an alternative.
  </p>

